hadiths[283]{hadithnumber,text}:
"116","Talhah bin Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), asking about Islam. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “(Islam) is to perform five prayers in the day and night.” He said: “Is there any prayer obligatory upon me besides this?” The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “No, except if you offer voluntary prayers.”"
"117","Talhah bin Ubaidullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A Bedouin from among the people of Najd came whose hair was disheveled, and the buzzing of his voice could be heard but it was not understood what he was saying. Until he came near, then it became clear that he was asking about Islam. So the Prophet (ﷺ) said: “(Islam is) to perform five prayers in the day and night.” He said: “Is there any prayer obligatory upon me besides this?” The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “No, except that you offer voluntary prayers.” And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned to him the fasting of Ramadan. He said: “Is there any other fasting obligatory upon me besides this?” The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “No, except that you observe voluntary fasts.” (The companion says) That man went back saying these words: “By Allah, I will neither add to this nor decrease from it.” The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “If he is truthful, he has succeeded.”"
"118","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: Jibreel (peace be upon him) led me in prayer twice at the House of Allah. The first time, he led the Zuhr prayer when the shadow (of things) was equal to the strap of a sandal. Then he led the Asr prayer when the shadow of everything became equal to its length, and Maghrib when the fasting person breaks his fast (i.e., at sunset). Then he led the Isha prayer when the redness (in the sky) disappeared. Then he led the Fajr prayer when eating and drinking became forbidden for the fasting person (i.e., at the time of dawn). Then, the second time, he led the Zuhr prayer when the shadow of everything became equal to its length, at the time when he had led the Asr prayer the previous day. Then he led the Asr prayer when the shadow of everything became twice its length. Then he led the Maghrib prayer at the same time as the first day. Then he led the Isha prayer when one third of the night had passed. Then he led the Fajr prayer when the earth had become well illuminated. Then Jibreel (peace be upon him) turned to me and said: O Muhammad (ﷺ)! This is the time of the Prophets before you, and the time for prayer is between these two times."
"119","It is narrated from Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him) that Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) delayed the prayer, so Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) said to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Jibreel came, led me in prayer, and I prayed with him... Then Jibreel came, led me in prayer, and I prayed with him. Then Jibreel came, led me in prayer, and I prayed with him. Then Jibreel came, led me in prayer, then Jibreel came, led me in prayer, and I prayed with him, until five prayers were counted."" Until five prayers were counted. So Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Urwah, fear Allah! What are you saying?"" Urwah said to Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him): ""Bashir bin Abi Mas'ud informed me from his father, who narrated from his father, and he narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"120","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would lead the morning prayer, and the women would return wrapped in their sheets, and due to the darkness, they would not be recognized."
"121","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: Believing women used to pray with the Prophet (ﷺ) and they would be wrapped in their cloaks, then they would return to their homes and due to the darkness, they would not be recognized."
"122","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would lead the morning prayer, the women would return wrapped in their sheets, and due to the darkness, they would not be recognized."
"123","A similar hadith is also narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) through another chain of transmission."
"124","Hibban bin Harith says: I came to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was at Abu Musa's house with the army. I saw that he was eating food. He said: ""Come, eat food."" I said, I intend to fast. So he said, I also had the same intention, then I also ate food. When we finished eating, he said: ""O son of Tiyah! Come, let us pray."
"125","It is narrated from Abu Barzah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) that while describing the way the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed prayer, he said: When you finished the morning prayer and turned back, none of us could recognize the person sitting next to him (because of the darkness), and the Prophet (ﷺ) would recite between sixty and one hundred verses."
"126","Ziyad bin Alaqah narrates from his uncle, he says: I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting « وَالنَّخْلَ بَاسِقَاتٍ » in the morning prayer. Imam Shafi’i, may Allah have mercy on him, says: That is, he used to recite Surah Qaf."
"127","It is narrated from Amr bin Huraith (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) reciting « وَاللَّيْلِ إِذَا عَسْعَسَ » in the Fajr prayer. Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) says: That is, in the Fajr prayer, he would recite « إِذَا الشَّمْسُ كُوِّرَتْ » (Surah At-Takweer)."
"128","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Sa’ib (may Allah be pleased with him), he states: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the morning prayer in Makkah. He began reciting Surah Al-Mu’minun, and when he reached the mention of Musa and Harun (peace be upon them), or the mention of Isa (peace be upon him), the Prophet (ﷺ) started coughing, so he left the recitation and went into bowing (ruku’). The narrator says that Abdullah bin Sa’ib (may Allah be pleased with him) was present in that prayer."
"129","Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) recited the entire Surah Al-Baqarah in both units of the morning prayer."
"130","Abdullah bin Amir says: We offered the morning prayer behind Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). In the first rak'ah, he recited Surah Yusuf, and in the second, Surah Al-Hajj, and he recited slowly and gently. The narrator says: I said, ""By Allah, then he must have performed that prayer at the time of sunrise."" So Abdullah bin Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""Yes."
"131","Farrafaṣah bin Umair al-Hanafi narrates: I learned the recitation of Surah Yusuf from Uthman bin Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) because he used to recite it frequently in the morning prayer."
"132","It is narrated from Rafi' bin Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Offer the Fajr prayer in brightness, you will receive more reward for it,"" or he said: ""There is more merit in it."
"133","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that he reports the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When the heat becomes intense, perform the prayer at a cooler time, for the severity of the heat is due to the boiling of the Hellfire."" And he said: ""The Hellfire complained to its Lord... (details)."
"134","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that he reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the heat becomes intense, perform the prayer at a cooler time, for the severity of heat is from the boiling of the Hellfire."" And he said: ""The Hellfire complained to its Lord and said: (Due to the intensity of the fire) some parts of me have consumed others. So Allah, the Exalted, allowed it two breaths: one breath in the winter and one breath in the summer. Thus, the extreme heat and extreme cold that you experience are because of this."
"135","From another chain of narration, from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), a narration similar to the previous hadith is reported."
"136","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the heat becomes intense, perform the prayer at a cooler time, for the severity of the heat is due to the boiling of the Hellfire."
"137","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that he states: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would perform the Asr prayer while the sun was still white and shining. Then, if someone set out towards Awali (the settlements located on the upper side outside of Madinah), he would reach there and the sun would still be high."
"138","It is narrated from Naufal bin Muawiyah Al-Daylami (may Allah be pleased with him) that he states: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever misses the Asr prayer, it is as if his wealth and his family have been destroyed."
"139","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: We used to perform the Maghrib prayer with the Prophet (peace be upon him), then go out for archery until we would enter the houses of Banu Salamah (tribe), and because of the light, we could see the place where the arrow fell."
"140","It is narrated from Zaid bin Khalid Al-Juhani (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: We used to offer the Maghrib prayer with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then on our return we would come to the market, and if an arrow was shot, its landing place would be visible."
"141","Qaqa' bin Hakim says: We came to Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: ""We used to pray (Maghrib) with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return to the houses of Banu Salamah, and we could still see the place where the arrows fell."
"142","It is narrated from Jubair bin Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I heard that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited Surah At-Tur in the Maghrib prayer."
"143","Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates that Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith (his mother) heard him reciting «والمرسلت عرفا» , so she said: ""O my son! By reciting this surah, you have reminded me that in the last part of his life, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reciting this same surah in Maghrib."
"144","Abu Abdullah Al-Sanabaji narrates that he came to Madinah during the caliphate of Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), and he prayed Maghrib behind Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him). In the first two rak‘ahs, he recited Surah Al-Fatihah and one surah each from the short Mufassal surahs. Then, when he stood for the third rak‘ah—(Abu Abdullah says) I came so close to him that my clothes were almost touching his clothes—I heard him reciting Surah Al-Fatihah and this verse: “Our Lord! Do not let our hearts deviate after You have guided us, and grant us mercy from Yourself. Surely, You are the Bestower.” (Aal-e-Imran: 8)"
"145","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates that Mu'adh bin Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform the (obligatory) 'Isha prayer with the Prophet ﷺ, then he would lead his people, Banu Salamah, in the same prayer. Jabir bin Abdullah says that the Prophet ﷺ delayed the 'Isha prayer one night, Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed with him, then returned and led his people in prayer, and began reciting Surah Al-Baqarah. A man separated himself from behind him and prayed alone. The people said to him: Have you become a hypocrite? The man said: No, but I will go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came to you and said: O Messenger of Allah! You led the prayer late, and Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed with you, then returned and led us in prayer and began with Surah Al-Baqarah. When I saw this, I separated and prayed alone, because we are farmers and work with our hands. The Prophet ﷺ turned to Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""O Mu'adh! Are you a cause of trial? Are you a cause of trial? Recite such and such surahs."
"146","In one chain of transmission of the previous narration from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), there is this addition that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: «سبح اسم ربك الاعلي» «والليل إذا يغشي» «والسماء والطارق» and recite similar Surahs to them. Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I said to Amr that Abu Zubair says that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: «سبح اسم ربك الاعلي» «والليل إذا يغشي» «والسماء والطارق» recite it, so Amr said: ""Yes, it is like that."
"147","Ibn Umar’s (may Allah be pleased with them) freed slave Nafi‘ (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) used to recite “When the earth is shaken” during prayer while traveling. He recited Surah Al-Fatihah, and when he began (the Surah), he said: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. (The narrator) Ayyub says, I asked: «اذا زلزلت الارض» So Nafi‘ said: (Yes) «اذا زلزلت الارض» ."
"148","Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Let not the Bedouins overpower you regarding the name of your prayer; its name is 'Isha.' These people call it 'Atamah' because they milk their she-camels in the darkness."
"149","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever catches one rak'ah of Fajr before the sun rises has caught the Fajr prayer, and whoever catches one rak'ah of Asr before the sun sets has caught the Asr prayer."
"150","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever catches one rak'ah of the prayer has caught the prayer."
"151","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) once slept through the morning prayer, so he performed it after the sun had risen and said: ""Whoever forgets a prayer should perform it when he remembers it, for Allah the Exalted says: 'And establish prayer for My remembrance.'"" [طه: 14]"
"152","A man from among the Companions of the Messenger ﷺ narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey. In the last part of the night, he ﷺ camped and said: “Is there any righteous person who will keep watch tonight, so that we do not oversleep and miss the prayer?” Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “I will (keep watch), O Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” It is said that Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) leaned against his mount and sat facing the east (so that he could call the adhan at the break of dawn). They woke up when the heat of the sun struck their faces. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Bilal!” Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! The same One who kept you from waking also kept me from waking.” The Companion says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution and prayed the two sunnah rak‘ahs of Fajr, then drove the camels a little further, and then performed the Fajr prayer."
"153","It is narrated from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: On the day of the Battle of the Trench, we were prevented from performing the prayer until a part of the night had passed after Maghrib, until Allah sufficed us from it, and this is the statement of Allah the Exalted: ""And Allah was sufficient for the believers in battle, and Allah is Strong and Mighty."" [احزاب: 25] The Prophet (ﷺ) called Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) and instructed him, so he gave the Iqamah for Zuhr, and the Prophet (ﷺ) led the Zuhr prayer in such a good manner as if he was praying it at its proper time. Then (Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him) gave the Iqamah for Asr, and the Prophet (ﷺ) led that prayer also in the same (good) manner. Then he gave the Iqamah for Maghrib and led the prayer in the same way. Then he gave the Iqamah for Isha and led that also in the same (good) manner. And this incident occurred before the verse regarding the prayer of fear was revealed: ""(If you fear) then [pray] on foot or riding."" [البقرة: 239]"
"154","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade performing prayer after Asr until the sun sets, and after Fajr until the sun rises."
"155","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""None of you should intend to pray at the time of sunrise or sunset."
"156","It is narrated from Abdullah al-Sanabaji (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the sun rises, the horns of Satan rise with it; when it ascends high, Satan separates from it. When it reaches the middle (of the sky), Satan joins it again. When the sun declines, he separates from it once more, and when it is near setting, he joins it again, and after sunset, he separates from it again. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) has prohibited performing prayer at these (three) times."
"157","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade praying at midday except on Friday, until the sun declines."
"158","Aamir bin Mus'ab narrates that Taawoos asked Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about performing two rak'ahs after the Asr prayer, so he forbade him. Taawoos said, ""I will still perform them."" So Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) recited the verse of the Qur'an: ""It is not for a believing man or woman, when Allah and His Messenger (ﷺ) have decided a matter, to have any choice in their affair."" [الاحزاب: 36]"
"159","Abu Salamah bin Abdur Rahman narrates that Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Madinah. He was seated on the pulpit when he said to Kathir bin Silat: ""Go to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) and ask her about the prayer after Asr of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and come back!"" Abu Salamah says: I also went with him to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), while Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) also sent Abdullah bin Harith bin Nawfal with us. He went to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) and asked her about it. So Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Kathir bin Silat: ""Go to Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) and ask her."" (Abu Salamah) says: I also went with him to Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) and asked her about it. Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""One day, after Asr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me and prayed two rak‘ahs which I had never seen him pray before. Then he said: 'I pray two rak‘ahs after Dhuhr, but today a delegation from Banu Tamim came to me, or some charity wealth came (the narrator is unsure), so I was unable to pray those rak‘ahs, so these are those (two rak‘ahs after Dhuhr).'"
"160","Abu Salamah narrates: Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Madinah. He was sitting on the pulpit when he said: ""O Kathir bin Salt! Go to the Mother of the Believers, Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), and ask her about the two rak‘ahs after ‘Asr that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray!"" Abu Salamah says: I also went with Kathir bin Salt, and Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) sent Abdullah bin Harith with us and said to him: ""Go and listen to what the Mother of the Believers says."" He went and asked Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), so Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""I do not have knowledge about this. Go to Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) and ask her about this issue."" Abu Salamah narrates: I also went with them to Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: ""One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to my house after ‘Asr and prayed two rak‘ahs, which I had not seen him pray before. I asked: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! Today you prayed a prayer that I have not seen you pray before? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I pray two rak‘ahs after Dhuhr, but a delegation from Banu Tamim came to me, or the charity of Banu Tamim, and they kept me busy from those rak‘ahs, so these are those (two rak‘ahs after Dhuhr).'"
"161","It is narrated from Qais (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw me performing two rak‘ahs after the morning prayer and said: ""O Qais, what are these two rak‘ahs?"" (Qais says) I replied: I had not performed the two Sunnah rak‘ahs of Fajr. (Upon hearing this) the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) remained silent."
"162","It is narrated from Jubair bin Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Banu Abd Manaf! Whoever among you becomes a ruler over the people, he should not prevent anyone from performing Tawaf of this House or praying in it at any time, day or night, whenever he wishes."
"163","Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated from another chain from the Prophet ﷺ a hadith similar to the previous one, except that in the chain of Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) there is this addition: ""O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, or O Banu Hashim, or O Banu Abd Manaf."
"164","It is narrated from Amr bin Dinar, he says: ""I saw Aban and Ata bin Abi Rabah, indeed Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) performed Tawaf of the House of Allah after the (Fajr) prayer and prayed before the sun had risen."
"165","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Muhairiz (may Allah have mercy on him), who was raised under the care of Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him) in his childhood due to being an orphan. When (Abu Mahdhurah) sent him to Syria, Abdullah bin Muhairiz (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I said to Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him): ""O uncle! I am going towards Syria, and I fear that I may be asked about your call to prayer (adhan), so please explain the matter to me."" Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Yes, I set out on a journey with a few people, we were on the road to Hunayn. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned (from the Battle of Hunayn) and we met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the way. At the encampment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his mu’adhdhin called the adhan for prayer, and we were averse to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We began to imitate the mu’adhdhin loudly, mocking him. The Prophet ﷺ heard our voices and sent men towards us, who brought us before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whose loud voice did I hear among you?' The whole group pointed towards me, and they were right. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept me and sent the rest away, and said: 'Stand and call the adhan for prayer!' I stood up, but my condition was such that I felt extreme dislike for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his command. I stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he himself taught me the adhan, saying: 'Say: ﴿Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar, Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah﴾.' Then he said: 'Raise your voice and say: ﴿Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah, Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah, Hayya 'ala-s-Salah, Hayya 'ala-s-Salah, Hayya 'ala-l-Falah, Hayya 'ala-l-Falah, Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar, La ilaha illa Allah﴾.' When I completed the adhan, he called me and gave me a pouch in which there was some silver, then he placed his hand on Abu Mahdhurah’s head, then passed it over his face, then over his chest, then over his liver, then his hand reached Abu Mahdhurah’s navel, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'May Allah bless you and bestow blessings upon you.' (Abu Mahdhurah says) I said: 'Appoint me to give the adhan in Makkah.' So he ﷺ said: 'I have appointed you.' Whatever dislike I had for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disappeared, and all of it turned into love for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I came to the governor of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, 'Attab bin Asid, and continued to call the adhan for prayer by the order of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Ibn Jurayj (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Everyone among the family of Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me just as Ibn Muhairiz did. Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I found Ibrahim bin Abdul Aziz bin Abdul Malik bin Abi Mahdhurah giving the adhan in the same way as Ibn Muhairiz narrated, and I heard him narrate it from his father, and his father from Ibn Abi Muhairiz, and he from Abu Mahdhurah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he from the Prophet ﷺ, in the same meaning as is transmitted from Ibn Jurayj."
"166","Imam Mujahid, regarding Allah Almighty’s statement ﴿وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ﴾ [الم نشرح: 4] , says: “Your mention will be raised along with Mine.” I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad (ﷺ) is the Messenger of Allah."
"167","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Abi Sa’sa’ah (may Allah have mercy on him) that Abu Sa’id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: “I see that you like sheep and the wilderness. When you are among your sheep or in the wilderness and you call the adhan for prayer, then raise your voice, for whatever hears the sound of your voice, whether a jinn, a human, or anything else, will bear witness for you on the Day of Resurrection.” Abu Sa’id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).”"
"168","It is narrated from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you hear the call to prayer (Adhan), say as the caller says."
"169","Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""When the muezzin said: 'Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah' (I bear witness that there is no god but Allah), the Prophet (ﷺ) also said: 'Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah.' And when he said: 'Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah' (I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah), the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'I also bear witness.' Then he remained silent."
"170","With another chain of narration, Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this same hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"171","Abdullah bin Alqamah bin Waqas narrates: When the muezzin of Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) gave the call to prayer, I was present with him. Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) repeated what his muezzin said (i.e., he responded to the call), until when the muezzin said: ""Hayya 'ala as-salah,"" he (Muawiyah) said: ""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah,"" and when he said: ""Hayya 'ala al-falah,"" he (Muawiyah) said: ""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah."" Then after that, he repeated the same words as the muezzin had said, then said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to do the same."
"172","Hafs bin Asim narrates that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man giving the adhan for Maghrib, so he responded to his adhan. Hafs says that the Prophet ﷺ went to the man who said ""Qad qamatis salah,"" so the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Come, perform the Maghrib prayer with the iqamah of this black slave."
"173","It is narrated from Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The callers to prayer are trustees over the prayers of the people."
"174","It is narrated from Yahya al-Mazni that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""All the earth is a mosque except for bathhouses and graveyards."" Imam Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, says: I have found this hadith in two places in my book, in one place as mursal (disconnected) and in another place narrated from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him. He reports it from the Prophet ﷺ."
"175","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Ma’qil or Mughfal (the narrator is unsure) that he reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: ""When the time for prayer comes to you in a sheepfold, then pray in it, for it is mercy and blessing. And when the time for prayer comes to you in a camel enclosure, then leave it and pray (outside), for it is created from the jinn. Do you not see that when it is angry, how loudly it bellows from its nose?"
"176","It is narrated from Uthman bin Abi Sulaiman that when the polytheists of Quraysh came to Madinah to pay ransom for the captives of Badr, they used to spend the night in the mosque. Among them was Jubair bin Mut'im as well. Jubair (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I used to listen to the recitation of the Prophet ﷺ."
"177","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""The people of Quba were offering the morning prayer when a man came and said that last night the Qur'an was revealed to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in which he was commanded to pray facing the Ka'bah. So the people turned their faces towards it. Their (the people of Quba's) faces were towards Syria, and they turned towards the Ka'bah (during the prayer)."
"178","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""The people were performing the Fajr prayer in Quba when a man came and informed them that during the night, Qur'an had been revealed to the Prophet ﷺ in which he was commanded to face the Ka'bah (in prayer). So those people also turned their faces towards the Ka'bah, while at that time their faces were towards Syria; they all turned towards the Ka'bah."
"179","Saeed bin Musayyib narrates: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed facing Bayt al-Maqdis for sixteen months, then two months before the Battle of Badr, the Qiblah was changed."
"180","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the Ka'bah, and with him were Bilal, Usamah, and Uthman bin Talhah (may Allah be pleased with them). Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him), ""What did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do inside the Ka'bah?"" Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: ""One pillar was on his left side and one on his right side, while three pillars were behind him, and he (ﷺ) performed prayer."" Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""At that time, the roof of the House of Allah was supported by six pillars."
"181","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered (the Ka'bah). Bilal, Uthman bin Talhah, and (the narrator says) I think he said Usama (may Allah be pleased with them) were also with him (ﷺ). When he (ﷺ) came out, I asked Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him), ""What did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do (inside the House of Allah)?"" He replied, ""He (ﷺ) placed one pillar on his right side, two pillars on his left side, and three pillars behind him, then he (ﷺ) prayed, and at that time the roof of the House of Allah was supported by six pillars."
"182","Narrated by Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray at night and I would be lying between him and the Qiblah in such a way as a body is placed in a bier (funeral)."
"183","It is narrated from Abu Juhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at the place of Abtah (performing prayer). Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) came with a spear and planted it, so the Prophet (ﷺ) performed prayer using it as a sutrah, while a dog, a woman, and a donkey would pass in front of him."
"184","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who states: ""I came riding on a donkey, and at that time I was close to reaching puberty, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was leading the people in prayer. I passed in front of the row, dismounted, and let the donkey graze, and then joined the row (of worshippers). No one objected to me for this."
"185","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""None of you should pray in a single garment in such a way that nothing is on his shoulders."
"186","It is narrated from Salamah bin Akwa’ (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: “I said, O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), we are out hunting, can we pray in a single shirt?” He (ﷺ) said: “Yes, and sew it. If you cannot do that, then fasten it with a thorn.”"
"187","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""None of you should pray in a garment in such a way that nothing is on his shoulder."
"188","It is narrated from the noble wife of the Prophet ﷺ, Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she states: ""The Prophet ﷺ would pray in a single garment, part of which would be on me and part on him, while I was in the state of menstruation."
"189","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) who says: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the mosque of Banu Amr bin Awf. He (ﷺ) was praying when some people from the Ansar came and greeted him (with salam) while he (ﷺ) was in the state of prayer. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked Suhaib (may Allah be pleased with him), how did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reply to them? He said: He (ﷺ) would gesture towards them."
"190","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who says: ""Before the migration to Abyssinia, we used to greet the Prophet ﷺ while he was in prayer, and he ﷺ would respond to us during the prayer. When we returned from Abyssinia, I came to the Prophet ﷺ to greet him. I found him ﷺ in prayer, so I greeted him, but he ﷺ did not respond to me. Various thoughts, both near and far, crossed my mind. I sat down until, when he ﷺ completed the prayer, I came to him ﷺ. He ﷺ said: Indeed, all praise is due to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. He brings about new commands in His affairs as He wills, and what Allah has newly commanded is that you should not speak during the prayer."
"191","Muhammad bin Ali bin Hanafiyyah narrates from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The key to prayer is ablution, its sanctification is the opening takbir (takbir tahrimah), and its conclusion is the salam."
"192","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he began the prayer, he would raise his hands until they were level with his shoulders, and when he intended to bow (ruku‘), and when he raised his head from bowing, (he would also raise his hands), and he would not raise (his hands) between the prostrations."
"193","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began the prayer, he would raise his hands up to his shoulders, and when he raised his head from bowing (ruku‘), he would raise his hands in the same manner. However, during prostrations (sujood), he (ﷺ) would not raise his hands."
"194","Nafi' narrates from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that when he started the prayer, he would raise his hands up to his shoulders, and when he raised his head from bowing (ruku'), he would raise both hands a little less than that."
"195","And with the same previous chain, it is narrated that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) when he started the prayer, he would raise his hands up to his shoulders, and when he rose from bowing (ruku‘), he would also raise his hands up to his shoulders in the same manner."
"196","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he began the prayer, he would raise his hands until they were level with his shoulders, and when he was about to bow (ruku‘) and when he raised his head from bowing, he would do the same (raise his hands), and he would not raise his hands between the two prostrations."
"197","Wa’il bin Hujr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), when he started the prayer, he would raise his hands up to his shoulders, and likewise when he bowed (for ruku‘) and when he raised his head from ruku‘ (he would also perform raf‘ al-yadayn). Wa’il (may Allah be pleased with him) says: “Then I came to him in the winter, and I saw (the noble Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, had wrapped themselves in sheets) and they would perform raf‘ al-yadayn from inside them.”"
"198","It is narrated from Bara’ bin Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he started the prayer, he would raise his hands. Sufyan says: Then I came to Kufa and met Yazid bin Abi Ziyad, and I heard him narrate this hadith with the addition that “then he would not return them.” I thought that this was “prompted” to him. Sufyan says: I heard Yazid narrate it in this way, then later I heard him narrate it with this addition as well, that “then he would not raise his hands.” Rabi’ bin Sulayman al-Muradi says: Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) considered Yazid to be mistaken in this hadith and said: It is as if he was prompted with this last phrase, which he adopted, and Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) did not consider Yazid to have a strong memory."
"199","It is narrated from Ali bin Hussain (may Allah be pleased with him) that he states: ""Whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would bow or rise in prayer, he would say the takbeer. This was always his way of praying until he departed to Allah."
"200","Abu Salamah narrates that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to lead the people in prayer, and when he would bow down and when he would rise up, he would say the takbeer. Then when he finished the prayer, he said: ""By Allah, among all of you, I am the one whose prayer most closely resembles the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."
"201","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would begin the prayer, he would recite: “I have turned my face in submission to the One who created the heavens and the earth, and I am not among the polytheists. Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my life, and my death are for Allah, the Lord of all the worlds. He has no partner, and I have been commanded to this, and I am among those who submit. O Allah! You are the King, there is no deity except You, You are pure with Your praise, You are my Lord and I am Your servant. I have wronged myself and I confess my sins, so forgive all my sins, for none forgives sins except You. Guide me to good character, for none guides to good character except You, and turn away bad character from me, for none can turn it away except You. O Allah, here I am, and all goodness is in Your hands, and evil is not attributed to You. The one whom You guide is truly guided. I am with You and turn to You. There is no refuge except with You. You are blessed and You are exalted. I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You.”"
"202","It is narrated from Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would begin the prayer, he would recite: ""I have turned my face towards Him who created the heavens and the earth, and I am not among the polytheists. Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my life, and my death are for Allah, the Lord of the worlds. He has no partner, and I have been commanded to do this."" Muslim bin Khalid and Abdul Majeed bin Abdul Aziz—one of them said: ""And I am the first of the Muslims,"" while the other said: ""And I am among the Muslims."" Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Then he would recite the Ta’awwudh (A‘udhu billah), then recite the Qur’an, then recite Bismillah ar-Rahman ar-Raheem. When he finished (Surah Al-Fatihah), he would say ""Ameen,"" and his followers would also say ""Ameen""! If he was the Imam, he would recite aloud in the audible prayers so that his followers would also hear his voice."
"203","Salih bin Abi Salih narrates that he heard Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) leading people in the obligatory prayer, reciting aloud: ""O our Lord! Indeed, we seek refuge in You from the accursed Satan,"" and likewise when he finished reciting Umm al-Qur'an (Al-Fatihah)."
"204","Salih narrates that Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) used to begin the prayer with ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Rahim."
"205","Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) led the prayer in Madinah. In it, he recited ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Rahim"" aloud with Surah Al-Fatihah, but did not recite ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Rahim"" aloud with the Surah after Al-Fatihah, until he completed its recitation. And when bowing (for ruku‘), he did not say the takbir aloud until he completed the prayer. When he finished with salam, those of the Muhajirun who heard this called out from every direction: ""O Muawiyah! Did you steal the prayer or did you forget?"" After that, when he led the prayer, he recited ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Rahim"" with the Surah after Al-Fatihah as well, and when he bowed for prostration, he said the takbir."
"206","It is narrated from Ubaid bin Rifaa that when Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Madinah, he led the prayer. He neither recited the “Bismillah ar-Rahman ar-Raheem” aloud nor said the takbeer when bowing and rising. When he finished the prayer with salam, the Muhajireen and Ansar called out to him: “O Muawiyah! You have stolen the prayer. Where is ‘Bismillah ar-Rahman ar-Raheem’? And when you bowed and rose, where was the takbeer?” Later, when he led another prayer, he said: This is the matter on which they criticized me."
"207","Ubaid bin Rifaa narrates from Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Muhajireen and Ansar, a narration similar to (the previous hadith) or with the same meaning, which does not contradict the (previous one). Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) says: In my view, this chain of narration is more reliable than the first chain."
"208","Nafi' narrates that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would not leave out ""Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem"" along with Surah Al-Fatihah and the following Surah."
"209","It is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who states that the Prophet (peace be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them) used to begin the recitation with ""Alhamdu lillahi Rabbil 'alamin."
"210","It is narrated from Sa'id bin Jubair, may Allah have mercy on him, regarding the verse: ﴿وَلَقَدْ آتَيْنَاكَ سَبْعًا مِنَ الْمَثَانِي وَالْقُرْآنَ الْعَظِيمَ﴾ [الحجر: 87] He says: ""By this is meant Umm al-Qur'an (Surah al-Fatiha)."" Ibn Jurayj says, my father said: Sa'id bin Jubair recited the complete (Surah al-Fatiha) to me and then said: 'Bismillah-ir-Rahman-ir-Raheem is its seventh verse.' Sa'id says: Just as I have recited to you, so did Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, recite it and say: 'Bismillah-ir-Rahman-ir-Raheem is its seventh verse.' Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: (Allah) has kept this preserved for you with Him and has not given it to anyone before you."
"211","It is narrated from ‘Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “The prayer of that person is not valid who did not recite Surah Al-Fatihah (in the prayer).”"
"212","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Every prayer in which Umm al-Kitab (Al-Fatiha) is not recited is incomplete, it is incomplete."
"213","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the Imam says Ameen, you should also say Ameen! Whoever's Ameen coincides with the Ameen of the angels, all his previous (minor) sins are forgiven."
"214","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the Imam says ﴿غير المغضوب عليهم ولا الضالين﴾, then you should say Ameen! Whoever's Ameen coincides with the Ameen of the angels, all his previous (minor) sins are forgiven."
"215","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When any one of you says Ameen, the angels in the heavens also say Ameen, and whoever's Ameen coincides with the Ameen of the angels, all his previous (minor) sins are forgiven."
"216","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the Imam says Ameen, you should also say Ameen. Whoever's Ameen coincides with the Ameen of the angels, all his previous sins are forgiven."" Ibn Shihab Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say Ameen."
"217","It is narrated from Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) that he states: I heard from the imams appointed by Abdullah bin Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them both) and the imams after them that they would say Ameen, and their followers would also say Ameen, to the extent that the mosque would resound."
"218","It is narrated from Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) who states: I heard the Imams and mentioned Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them both) and the era after him, that the (Imam) would say Ameen and their followers would also say Ameen, to the extent that the mosque would resound."
"219","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when he prayed four rak'ahs alone, would recite Surah Al-Fatihah and a surah from the Qur'an in every rak'ah. Nafi' says, and sometimes in one rak'ah of an obligatory prayer, he would recite two or three surahs as well."
"220","It is narrated from Rifaa bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""When any of you stands for prayer, he should perform ablution as Allah has commanded him, then say the takbeer, and if he knows any Qur'an, he should recite from it, and if he does not know anything from the Qur'an, then he should praise Allah, and say Allahu Akbar and bow in rukoo' until he is at ease in rukoo', then stand up until he is at ease standing, then prostrate until he is at ease in prostration, then raise his head and sit until he is at ease sitting. Whoever falls short in this has indeed fallen short in his prayer."
"221","It is narrated from Rifaa’ah bin Rafi’ (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man entered the mosque and began to pray near the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). (When he completed his prayer) he came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and greeted him with salam. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “Repeat your prayer, for you have not prayed.” The man stood up and prayed in the same manner as before. The Prophet (ﷺ) again said: “Repeat your prayer, for you have not prayed.” He said: O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! Teach me, how should I pray? The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “When you stand for prayer, face the qiblah and say Allahu Akbar, then recite Surah Al-Fatihah and whatever (portion) of the Qur’an Allah has made easy for you. When you bow (in ruku’), place your palms on your knees, perform your ruku’ with calmness, and straighten your back. When you rise, straighten your back and raise your head until the bones return to their joints. When you prostrate, prostrate with calmness, and when you rise, sit on your left thigh. Then perform every ruku’ and prostration with calmness in the same way.”"
"222","It is narrated from Rifaa bin Rafi’ (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to a man: ""When you bow (in ruku’), place your palms on your knees and remain firm in your bowing, and when you rise, straighten your back and raise your head until every bone returns to its place."
"223","It is narrated from Nu'man bin Murrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""What do you say about the drunkard, the adulterer, and the thief?"" And this statement was before the revelation of the prescribed punishments for these crimes. The noble Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) said: Allah and His Messenger know best. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""These are evils and there is punishment for them, and the worst thief is the one who steals from the prayer."" Then the hadith continues further."
"224","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I have been forbidden from reciting the Qur'an while in the state of bowing and prostration. Therefore, glorify the greatness of your Lord in bowing, and strive and seek in supplication during prostration. For it is more likely that (your supplication) will be accepted."
"225","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""Beware! I have been forbidden from reciting the Qur'an while in the state of bowing and prostration. In bowing, glorify the greatness of your Lord, and in prostration, make abundant supplication, for your supplication is more worthy of being accepted."
"226","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would bow in rukū‘, he would recite: ""O Allah! I have bowed for You, and I have submitted to You, and I have believed in You, and You alone are my Lord. My ears, my eyes, my bones, my hair, my skin, and (this body) which my feet have lifted, have all humbled themselves before Allah, the Lord of all the worlds."
"227","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Prophet (ﷺ) would bow (in rukū‘), he would recite: ""O Allah! I have bowed for You, and I have believed in You, and I have submitted to You, and You alone are my Lord. My ears, my eyes, my mind, my bones, and my body—whatever my feet have carried—have humbled themselves for You, for Allah, the Lord of all the worlds."
"228","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""When you bow (in ruku‘), recite: 'O Allah! I have bowed for You, and I have humbled myself for You, and I have submitted to Your obedience, and I have believed in You, and I have placed my trust in You, so my bowing is complete for You.'"
"229","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Prophet (ﷺ) would raise his head from bowing in an obligatory prayer, he would recite: ""O Allah! O our Lord! All praise is for You, as much as would fill the heavens and the earth and as much as would fill whatever else You will."
"230","It is narrated from Awn bin Abdullah bin Uqbah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you bows (in ruku') and recites ﴿Subhana Rabbiyal 'Azim﴾ three times, his ruku' is complete, and this is the minimum. And when he prostrates (in sujud) and recites ﴿Subhana Rabbiyal A'la﴾ three times, his sujud is complete, and this is the minimum."
"231","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed prostration, he would say: ""O Allah! For You I have prostrated, and to You I have submitted, and in You I have believed, and You alone are my Lord. My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned its hearing and sight. Blessed is Allah, the Best of creators."
"232","It is narrated from Muhammad (al-Baqir), may Allah have mercy on him, that people who collected firewood came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah! We are always on a journey, what should we do about the prayer? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Recite three tasbihs in the bowing (ruku‘) and three tasbihs in the prostration (sajdah)."
"233","It is narrated from Awn bin Abdullah bin Uqbah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you bows (in ruku') and recites ﴿Subhana Rabbiyal 'Azim﴾ three times, his ruku' is complete, and this is the minimum number. And when he prostrates (in sujood) and recites ﴿Subhana Rabbiyal A'la﴾ three times, his sujood is complete, and this is the minimum number."
"234","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to recite between the two prostrations: ""O Allah! Forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, and remove my shortcomings."
"235","Ja'far bin Muhammad narrates from his father that he said: ""When the news of the killing of the people of Bi'r Ma'unah reached the Prophet ﷺ, he stayed for fifteen nights; whenever he would raise his head from the last rak'ah in the morning prayer, he would say: 'Sami' Allahu liman hamidah, Rabbana lakal hamd' (then) 'O Allah! You do...'—he recited a long supplication, then he would say the takbir and go into prostration."
"236","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his head from the bowing in the second rak'ah of the morning prayer, he supplicated: ""O Allah! Deliver Walid bin Walid, Salamah bin Hisham, 'Ayyash bin Abi Rabi'ah, and other weak Muslims in Makkah. O Allah! Make Your grip severe upon the people of the tribe of Mudar. O Allah! Send upon them a famine like that in the time of Yusuf (peace be upon him)."
"237","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) recited Qunoot in the morning prayer and made this supplication: ""O Allah! Grant salvation to Waleed bin Waleed, Salamah bin Hisham, and Ayyash bin Abi Rabee'ah."
"238","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) did not recite Qunoot in any prayer."
"239","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet (ﷺ) was commanded to prostrate on seven parts: both hands, both knees, the sides of the toes (of the feet), and the forehead, and he was forbidden from gathering his hair and clothes. Ibn Tawus narrated additional words: that he placed his hand on the forehead and nose. (Ibn Tawus says) My father used to count the nose and forehead as one."
"240","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) was commanded to prostrate on seven parts and was forbidden from gathering his hair and clothes (during prostration)."
"241","It is narrated from Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), son of Abdul Muttalib, that he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) saying: ""When a servant prostrates, seven parts of his body prostrate along with him: his face, his hands, his knees, and his two feet."
"242","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) was commanded to prostrate on seven parts. Among them, he also mentioned both his palms and both his knees."
"243","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when he performed prostration, would place his palms at the spot where he placed his face. Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) says: I saw him in severe cold, that he would take his hands out from his cloak (and place them on the ground)."
"244","It is narrated from Hasan (may Allah have mercy on him), who narrates from his mother, that she said: I saw Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, prostrating on a pillow made of leather."
"245","It is narrated from Mujahid (may Allah have mercy on him) who states: A servant is closest to Allah while in the state of prostration. Have you not reflected on Allah’s saying: “Prostrate and draw near,” that is, prostrate and attain closeness to Allah."
"246","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Aqram Al-Khuzai (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prostrating in the plain of Namirah, and I saw the whiteness of his armpits."
"247","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Aqram Al-Khuzai (may Allah be pleased with him) who says: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prostrating in the plain of Namirah, and I saw the whiteness of his armpits."
"248","It is narrated from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) performed prostration, due to his sides being separated from his body, if a small (goat or sheep) kid wanted to pass underneath him, it could pass."
"249","It is narrated from Abu Qilabah (may Allah have mercy on him) that he stated: Malik bin Huwairith (may Allah be pleased with him) came to us. He prayed in our mosque and said: ""By Allah! I would not have prayed, nor did I intend to pray, but I wanted to show you how I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying."" He stated that he (ﷺ) would stand from the first rak'ah, and when he intended to rise, Abu Qilabah says, I asked: How? (Did he bend?) Malik bin Huwairith replied: ""Like the way I performed this prayer."
"250","It is narrated from Abu Qilabah, may Allah have mercy on him, in a manner similar to the previous hadith, except that in this one he said: ""When Malik bin Huwairith, may Allah be pleased with him, would raise his head from the last prostration of the first rak'ah, he would sit upright (i.e., perform the sitting of rest), then he would stand up by leaning on the ground."
"251","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to sit (for Tashahhud) after the first two rak'ahs as if he were sitting on a hot stone."" Imam Shafi'i said: ""Until he would stand up?"" Ibrahim (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Yes! That is what he intended to convey."
"252","It is narrated from Abu Humayd As-Sa'idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat after two rak'ahs, he would spread his left foot and sit on it, and keep his right foot upright. And when he sat after four rak'ahs, he would move his feet away from his buttocks and sit on the ground with his buttocks, and place his right buttock on the ground."
"253","It is narrated from Ali bin Abdur Rahman Al-Mu'awi, he states: ""Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saw me playing with pebbles. When he finished the prayer, he forbade me and said: Do as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to do."" Ali bin Abdur Rahman says, I asked: ""How did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do?"" Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) replied: ""When the Prophet (ﷺ) sat in prayer (for Tashahhud), he would place his right palm on his right thigh, then close all his fingers and point with the finger next to the thumb (the index finger), and place his left palm on his left thigh."
"254","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach us the Tashahhud just as he used to teach us the Qur'an, and he (ﷺ) would say: ""All blessed words, physical acts, and wealth are for Allah. O Prophet (ﷺ)! Peace be upon you, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad (ﷺ) is the Messenger of Allah."
"255","It is narrated from Abdur Rahman bin Abdu Qari that he heard Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) teaching people the Tashahhud from the pulpit. He was saying: ""You should recite: All verbal, financial, and physical acts of worship are for Allah. O Prophet! Peace be upon you, and the mercy and blessings of Allah. And peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad (ﷺ) is His servant and Messenger."
"256","Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) says: I heard Ibn Abbas and Ibn Zubair (may Allah be pleased with them) and they did not differ in the Tashahhud. (That is, both narrated the same words from the Prophet ﷺ.)"
"257","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he asked: O Messenger of Allah! How should we send blessings upon you in prayer? The Prophet ﷺ replied: ""Say: O Allah! Send mercy upon Muhammad ﷺ and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent mercy upon Ibrahim (peace be upon him), and send blessings upon Muhammad ﷺ and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim (peace be upon him). Then send peace upon me."
"258","It is narrated from Ka'b bin 'Ujrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to recite in prayer: ""O Allah! Send mercy upon Muhammad (ﷺ) and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent mercy upon Ibrahim (peace be upon him) and upon the family of Ibrahim, and send blessings upon Muhammad (ﷺ) and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim (peace be upon him) and upon the family of Ibrahim. Surely, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious."
"259","Aamir bin Saeed narrates from his father (Sa’d bin Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him) that he narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that when he ﷺ finished the prayer, he would turn (his head) to the right and to the left and say salam."
"260","From another chain, ‘Amir bin Sa‘eed narrated from his father that he reported a narration similar to the previous hadith from the Prophet ﷺ."
"261","It is narrated from Wathilah bin Asqa’ (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) would turn to his right and left while saying salam, until his cheeks would become visible."
"262","It is narrated from Sahl bin Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Prophet (ﷺ) finished the prayer, he would turn his face to the right and to the left and say salam."
"263","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) would turn to the right and to the left when saying salam (at the end of prayer)."
"264","It is narrated from Wasi' bin Hibban that on one occasion he mentioned the name of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and on another occasion Abdullah bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him). They narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would turn his face to the right and to the left while saying salam."
"265","It is narrated from Jabir bin Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and when he finished the prayer with salam, some of us would gesture with their hands to the right and left, saying, ""As-salamu Alaikum, As-salamu Alaikum."" Jabir bin Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet (ﷺ) gestured with his hand to the right and left (to show the companions). Observing this, the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""You gesture with your hands as the tails of mischievous horses move. Is it not sufficient for any of you? Or is it sufficient for you (the narrator is in doubt) that you place your hands on your thighs (in the sitting position), then turn your face to the right and left and say: As-salamu Alaikum wa Rahmatullah."
"266","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he states: ""I used to recognize the conclusion of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) by the sound of the takbeer (Allahu Akbar)."" Amr bin Dinar (may Allah have mercy on him) says: ""Later, I mentioned this hadith to Abu Ma'bad, and he said: I did not narrate this hadith to you,"" Amr says: ""Even though he was the one who narrated it to me,"" and he said: ""He is the most truthful among the freed slaves of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him)."" Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) says: ""It is as if he forgot after narrating this hadith to them."
"267","Abdullah bin Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), after concluding the prayer with salam, would recite aloud: ""There is no deity except Allah, He is alone, He has no partner, to Him belongs the dominion and to Him belongs all praise, and He is over all things competent. Abstaining from sins and having the ability to worship is only possible through Allah’s help. And we worship only Him, every blessing belongs to Him, and all bounty is His possession, all good praise is for Him. There is no (true) deity except Allah, we (only) worship Him even if the disbelievers dislike it."
"268","It is narrated from the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she stated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would finish the prayer with salam, the women would stand up as soon as the salam was completed, and the Prophet ﷺ would remain seated in his place for a short while. Ibn Shihab said: We think that he ﷺ did so for this reason—and full knowledge is with Allah alone—so that the women could leave, and the men would not reach them after finishing the prayer."
"269","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to turn to the right and to the left after the prayer."
"270","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who says: ""None of you should ever allocate a share for Satan in his prayer by considering it necessary to turn only to the right side, whereas I have often seen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turning to his left side."
"271","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Offering prayer in congregation is twenty-seven degrees more virtuous than a man's (individual) prayer."
"272","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Performing prayer in congregation is twenty-five times more virtuous than performing prayer alone among you."
"273","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was in Baqi', and when he heard the iqamah, he hurried towards the mosque."
"274","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""By Him in Whose hand is my soul: I intended to order for firewood to be collected, then order for the call to prayer to be given, then appoint someone to lead the people in prayer, then I would go to those men (who do not attend the prayer) and burn their houses over them. By Him in Whose hand is my soul, if any one of them knew that he would get a meaty bone or two good shanks, he would surely come to the ‘Isha prayer."
"275","It is narrated from Abdur Rahman bin Harmalah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The distinction between us and the hypocrites is attending the congregational prayer of Isha and Fajr. (Because) they do not have the ability to attend these two (prayers in congregation)."
"276","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: When the night was cold and windy, the Prophet ﷺ would instruct the muezzin to say in the adhan: ""Ala sallu fi r-rihal"" (Be aware! Pray in your homes!)."
"277","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) once gave the call to prayer on a cold and windy night and said: "" ألا صلوا فى الرحال (Pray in your dwellings)."" Then he said: When the night was cold and rainy, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would order the mu'adhdhin to say in the call to prayer: "" ألا صلوا فى الرحال (Pray in your dwellings)."
"278","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would instruct his muezzin on a rainy and windy cold night to say in the call to prayer: "" ألا صلوا في الرحال "" (Pray in your dwellings)."
"279","It is narrated from Mihjan (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when the call to prayer was given. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up and performed the prayer, while Mihjan remained seated in his place. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) asked him: ""Why did you not pray with us? Are you not a Muslim?"" Mihjan replied: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! I am a Muslim, but I had already performed the prayer at home."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said: ""When you come (to the mosque), pray with the people, even if you have already performed the prayer."
"280","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Whoever has performed the Maghrib and Fajr prayers, and then joins these prayers with the Imam as well, he should by no means repeat them."
"281","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) that Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) led his people in the 'Isha prayer and began reciting Surah Al-Baqarah. A man separated himself from the congregation and prayed alone. He mentioned this to the Prophet (ﷺ), so the Prophet (ﷺ) said to Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Are you one who puts people to trial? Are you one who puts people to trial? Recite such-and-such Surah."
"282","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he reports from the Prophet (peace be upon him) a narration similar to the previous hadith. And at the end of this hadith, it is mentioned that Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I narrated this hadith to Amr, and he said: ""Yes, it is exactly like that."
"283","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""When any of you leads the prayer, he should make it light, because there are sick and elderly people in the congregation. But when he is praying alone, he may make it as long as he wishes."
"284","It is narrated from Hammam bin Harith (may Allah have mercy on him), he states: Hudhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) led us in prayer on a raised platform, he came and prayed on it, so Abu Mas’ud Badri pulled him, and Hudhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) followed him. When the prayer was completed, Abu Sa’id (may Allah be pleased with him) inquired: “Has this not been prohibited?” So Hudhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Did you not see that I obeyed you?”"
"285","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that his grandmother, Malikah (may Allah be pleased with her), invited the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for a meal which she had prepared as a hospitality for him. The Prophet (ﷺ) ate the food, then said: ""Come, let me lead you in prayer."" Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We stood on a mat which had turned black due to its oldness. I cleaned it with water, then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood on it, and I and the orphan (Dhumairah bin Sa'd) formed a row behind him, and (my) elderly (grandmother) was behind us."
"286","It is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he states: I and a small child offered prayer behind the Prophet (ﷺ) in our house, and Umm Sulaym (may Allah be pleased with her) was behind us."
"287","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that his grandmother, Malikah (may Allah be pleased with her), invited the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for a meal which she had prepared as a treat for him. The Prophet (ﷺ) ate the food, then said: ""Come, let me lead you in prayer."" Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I stood towards a mat which had turned black due to frequent use in our house. I sprinkled water on it to clean it, then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood on it. I and a small boy formed a row behind him, and the old woman stood behind us. The Prophet (ﷺ) led us in two rak‘ahs of prayer, then departed."
"288","Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I and a small orphan boy prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and Umm Sulaim (may Allah be pleased with her) was behind us."
"289","Wabisah bin Ma’bad (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saw a man praying alone behind the row and ordered him to repeat the prayer."
"290","It is narrated from Salih, the freed slave of Al-Tuwamah, may Allah have mercy on him, who states: I saw Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he performed prayer behind the Imam alone on the roof of the mosque."
"291","It is narrated from Salih bin Ibrahim who states: I saw Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) perform the Friday prayer in congregation behind the Imam in the houses of Humaid bin Abdur Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him); the Imam was in the mosque. There was a road between Humaid's houses and the mosque."
"292","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The imam is responsible for the prayer of those who follow him, and the muezzin is trustworthy. Allah has guided the imams and forgiven the muezzins."
"293","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The imam is responsible for the prayer of those who follow him, and the muezzin is trustworthy. O Allah! Guide the imams, and forgive the muezzins."
"294","Malik bin Huwairith (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us: ""Pray as you have seen me praying, and when the time for prayer comes, let one of you give the call to prayer (adhan), and let the eldest among you lead the prayer."
"295","Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said that it is established from the Sunnah that a resident person should lead the prayer."
"296","Ubaid bin Umair (may Allah have mercy on him) says: People gathered around Mecca. Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) says: In my opinion, they said: Here in the high valley at the time of Hajj. Ubaid says: When the time for prayer came, a man from the family of Abu Sa’ib, who was not eloquent in speech, stepped forward. Miswar bin Makhrama (may Allah be pleased with him) pushed him back and put someone else forward. This matter reached Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), but he did not ask Miswar (may Allah be pleased with him) about it until they came to Madinah. When they arrived in Madinah, he inquired about it. Miswar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Look, O Commander of the Faithful! That man was not eloquent in speech and it was the season of Hajj. I feared that some pilgrims would hear his recitation and adopt his lack of eloquence (i.e., memorize it incorrectly).” So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Is that why you did it?” Miswar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I said: “Yes.” So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “You did the right thing.”"
"297","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that at the time of the martyrdom of Abdullah bin Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him), Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) went aside to Mina. When Hajjaj was in Mina, he (Ibn Umar) performed prayer under the leadership of Hajjaj."
"298","It is narrated from Ja'far bin Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him) that Hasan and Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to pray behind Marwan. Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) says, Hatim bin Isma'il asked: ""Did they not come to their homes and (repeat) the prayer?"" So Ja'far (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""No, by Allah, they did not add to the prayer of the imams."
"299","It is narrated from Mahmood bin Rabee’ (may Allah have mercy on him) that ‘Itbaan bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) was the imam of his people and he was blind. He submitted to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): “I am a blind man and (sometimes) there is darkness, rain, and flood, so O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! Please pray at some place in my house so that I may make it a place of prayer.” Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came and said: “Where would you like me to pray?” He pointed to a place in the house, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed there."
"300","It is narrated from Mahmood bin Rabee’ (may Allah have mercy on him) that ‘Itbaan bin Malik Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) was a blind companion and used to lead his people in prayer."
"301","Abdullah bin Ubaidullah bin Abi Mulaykah narrates that he, Ubaid bin Umair, Miswar bin Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with them both), and many other people would come to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) from the upper part of the valley. Aisha’s freed slave, Abu Amr, would lead them in prayer. At that time, Abu Amr was still her slave and had not yet been freed. And it was said: He (Abu Amr) was the imam of the sons of Muhammad bin Abi Bakr and Urwah."
"302","Nafi’ (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that the iqamah was pronounced in a mosque near Madinah, and Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) had land near that mosque in which he used to farm, and the imam of that mosque was a freed slave of Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). That freed slave and his companions resided there. Nafi’ says: When Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) heard their (adhan and iqamah), he came to pray with them. The freed slave of that mosque said to Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): “Come, lead us in prayer.” ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: “You have more right to lead the prayer in your mosque.” Then the freed slave led the prayer."
"303","It is narrated from Hujairah that Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) led them in prayer while standing in the middle."
"304","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) would perform the 'Isha prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then go to his own people and lead them in the same prayer. For Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him), this prayer would be voluntary, while for his people it would be the obligatory 'Isha prayer."
"305","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) that Mu'adh bin Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform the (obligatory) 'Isha prayer with the Prophet (peace be upon him), then he would go to his people and lead them in the 'Isha prayer, and for him, this would be a voluntary prayer."
"306","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted a horse and fell from it, injuring his right side. He (ﷺ) led a prayer sitting, so we also prayed sitting behind him. When he (ﷺ) finished, he said: ""The Imam is appointed to be followed, so when he prays standing, you should also pray standing, and when he bows, you should also bow, and when he raises his head from bowing, you should also raise your heads, and when he says ﴿Sami' Allahu liman hamidah﴾, you should say ﴿Rabbana wa laka al-hamd﴾, and when he prays sitting, you should all pray sitting."
"307","From another chain of narration, it is also reported from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) in the same manner (as the previous hadith)."
"308","Narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she states: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed prayer in my house, and he (ﷺ) was ill. He (ﷺ) prayed sitting, and the people behind him prayed standing, so he (ﷺ) gestured to them to sit down. When he (ﷺ) finished the prayer, he said: “The imam is appointed so that he may be followed. When he bows, you should bow; and when he raises his head, you should raise your heads; and when he prays sitting, you should also pray sitting.”"
"309","It is narrated from Abu Zubair that he reports from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that they went out while Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) was bidding them farewell as he was ill. Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) led the prayer sitting, so they also prayed sitting behind him."
"310","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer. When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) felt better, he came and sat down beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in prayer while he was sitting, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer while standing."
"311","Ubaid bin Umair Al-Laithi narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in the morning prayer, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) pronounced the takbeer. The Prophet ﷺ felt some relief, stood up, and came forward past the rows. Ubaid says that when Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) led the prayer, he would not turn to any side. When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sensed something behind him and realized that no one except the Messenger of Allah ﷺ could come to that place, he stepped back towards the row, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned him to his place. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat beside him, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) remained standing, until when Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) finished the prayer, he said: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! I thought you had recovered, and this day belonged to the daughter of Kharijah. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) returned to his house, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat in his place at one side of the chamber, warning the people against tribulations. He ﷺ said: ""People should not follow my example in every matter, for I declare lawful what Allah has made lawful in His Book, and I declare unlawful what Allah has made unlawful in His Book. O Fatimah! Daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, O Safiyyah! Aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, act for what is with Allah, for surely I cannot avail you anything against Allah."
"312","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was ill, he ordered Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer. When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) felt some relief, he came (to the mosque) and sat beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in prayer while sitting, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer while standing."
"313","It is narrated from Ubaid bin Umair (may Allah have mercy on him) through another chain, who reports from the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) in the same meaning without any difference."
"314","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came out during his illness and went to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was standing and leading the people in prayer. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) began to step back, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) signaled to him to remain as he was standing. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sat down beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was following the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), while the people were following Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"315","Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) a hadith with the same meaning, which does not differ from it and is even clearer, and said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) stood by the side of the Prophet ﷺ and performed the prayer."
"316","It is narrated from Ubaid bin Umair, may Allah have mercy on him, who states: A trustworthy person informed me (as if he meant Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her). Then he mentioned the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ and that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was at his side, similar to the narration of Hisham bin Urwah from his father."
"317","It is narrated from Sahl bin Sa’d Al-Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went to the people of Banu Amr bin Awf to make peace among them. The time for the Asr prayer came, and the muezzin came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). Abu Bakr As-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) stepped forward to lead the prayer. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived, and the people began to clap in abundance. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) would not turn to any side during the prayer, but when the people started clapping excessively, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) turned and saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) signaled to him to remain in his place, but Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) raised his hands and thanked Allah for the honor that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had granted him the leadership of the prayer. Then he stepped back, and the Prophet (ﷺ) moved forward and led the prayer. When the prayer was finished, he said to the people: “What is the matter? I saw you clapping a lot. If something arises in the prayer, one should say ‘Subhan Allah’. When he says it, attention will be paid to him, and clapping is for women.”"
"318","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""For men, it is to say 'Subhan Allah', and for women, it is to clap."
"319","It is narrated from Sahl bin Sa’d Al-Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went to reconcile between the Banu Amr bin Awf. The time for prayer came, and the muezzin said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): “Will you lead the people in prayer, and should I pronounce the iqamah?” Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Yes.” So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) began the prayer. Meanwhile, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived while the people were praying. He (ﷺ) passed through the people until he reached the first row. The people started clapping their hands. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) would not pay attention to anything during the prayer, but when the people began to clap repeatedly, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) turned and saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) signaled to him to remain in his place (and lead the prayer). Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) raised his hands and praised Allah for the honor that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had granted him to lead the prayer, then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) stepped back, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) moved forward and led the prayer. When the prayer was finished, he said: “O Abu Bakr! When I instructed you, why did you not remain in your place?” Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: “It was not befitting for the son of Abu Quhafah to lead the prayer in front of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “It is strange that I saw you all clapping so much. If anyone encounters an issue during the prayer, he should say ‘Subhan Allah,’ for when he says ‘Subhan Allah,’ attention will be paid to him. And clapping is for women.”"
"320","It is narrated from Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) would sometimes lead the prayer while carrying Umamah bint Abi Al-As (may Allah be pleased with her). Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Umamah’s clothes are the clothes of a child."
"321","It is narrated from Abu Qatadah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) would sometimes lead the people in prayer while carrying Umamah bint Zainab (may Allah be pleased with her). When he (peace be upon him) would go into prostration, he would put her down, and when he stood up, he would pick her up."
"322","It is narrated from Abu Qatadah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet ﷺ (sometimes) would pray and he would be carrying Umamah bint Abi Al-As, and she was the granddaughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he would prostrate, he would put her down, and when he stood up, he would pick her up."
"323","It is narrated from Ata bin Yasar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) pronounced the takbeer in one of the prayers, then gestured with his hand, ""Wait,"" then returned, and there were traces of water on his skin."
"324","Through another chain, a narration with the same meaning as the previous hadith is reported from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet ﷺ."
"325","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) that he was leading his companions in prayer one day, then he went to relieve himself (appointing someone to lead in his place), and when he returned, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say, ""When any of you feels the need to relieve himself, he should do so before performing the prayer."
"326","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) that he set out towards Makkah and some people from his tribe accompanied him. He used to lead them in prayer. (Once) the prayer was established and he took hold of a man and put him forward, and said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When the congregation is established and any of you feels the need to relieve himself, he should first relieve himself."
"327","Abdullah bin Buhaina (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in two rak‘ahs of a (four-rak‘ah) prayer, then he stood up without sitting (for tashahhud), and the people also stood up with him. When he completed the prayer, we began to wait for him to say the salam, but before saying the salam, he performed two prostrations while he was sitting, and then he said the salam."
"328","It is narrated from Ibn Buhaina (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood up after two rak‘ahs in the Zuhr prayer and did not sit (i.e., did not recite the tashahhud). When he completed the prayer, he performed two prostrations first and then said the salam."
"329","It is narrated from Ibn Buhaina (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in (a four-unit prayer) and prayed two units, then stood up without sitting for the first tashahhud, and the people also stood up with him. When the prayer was completed, we began to wait for him to say the salam. He said ""Allahu Akbar"" and, before the salam, performed two prostrations while sitting."
"330","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that after leading two rak‘ahs, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned back, and Dhu’l-Yadayn (the one with long arms) said to him: “Did you forget, or has the prayer been shortened?” The Prophet (ﷺ) asked the people: “Is Dhu’l-Yadayn speaking the truth?” The people said: “Yes.” Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up and led the remaining two rak‘ahs, then gave salam, then said takbeer ﴿الله اكبر﴾ and performed a prostration as long as or longer than the previous prostrations, then raised his head, then said takbeer and performed another prostration as long as or longer than the previous prostrations, then raised his head."
"331","Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the ‘Asr prayer. He ended the prayer with salam after two rak‘ahs, so Dhu’l-Yadayn stood up and asked: “O Messenger of Allah! Has the prayer been shortened or did you forget?” The Prophet (ﷺ) turned (to the people for confirmation) and asked: “Is Dhu’l-Yadayn speaking the truth?” The people said: “Yes.” The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then completed the remainder of the prayer, and after the salam, performed two prostrations while sitting."
"332","Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (ﷺ) concluded the ‘Asr prayer after three rak‘ahs. Then he stood up and went into his chamber. Khirbaq, who was a man with long arms, stood up and called out: “O Messenger of Allah! Has the prayer been shortened?” The Prophet (ﷺ) came out dragging his cloak in anger, asked the people, and was informed (that he had led three rak‘ahs). He then completed the missed rak‘ah, performed two prostrations, and (afterwards) concluded with salam."
"333","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) recited Surah An-Najm, he (peace be upon him) performed prostration and everyone except two men performed prostration with him. He (peace be upon him) said: ""These two sought fame."
"334","It is narrated from A’raj that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) recited Surah (By the star when it goes down), then he performed a prostration in it, and then stood up and recited another Surah."
"335","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Thalabah bin Sa’eer that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) led them in prayer at the place of Jabiya, recited Surah Al-Hajj, and performed two prostrations in it."
"336","It is narrated from Abu Salamah bin Abdur Rahman that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) recited Surah ﴿إِذَا السَّمَاءُ انْشَقَّتْ﴾ for them and performed a prostration in it. When he finished, he informed them that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) also performed a prostration in it."
"337","It is narrated from Ata bin Yasar that a man recited a verse of prostration before the Prophet ﷺ and performed prostration, so the Noble Prophet ﷺ also performed prostration. Then another person recited a verse of prostration before him, but the Prophet ﷺ did not perform prostration. That man said: ""O Messenger of Allah! So-and-so recited a verse of prostration before you and you performed prostration, but when I recited it, you did not."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""You were the imam; if you had performed prostration, I would have performed prostration as well."
"338","It is narrated from Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) that he recited Surah An-Najm before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he (peace be upon him) did not perform prostration in it."
"339","It is narrated from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that he did not perform prostration in Surah (Sad) and said: This is merely the mention of a prophet's repentance."
"340","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) traveled between Makkah and Madinah in a state of security; he did not fear anyone except Allah, yet he performed two rak‘ahs of prayer (i.e., he shortened the prayer)."
"341","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) traveled between Makkah and Madinah in a state of security; he did not fear anyone except Allah Almighty, and he would perform two rak‘ahs of prayer."
"342","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) traveled between Makkah and Madinah in a state of security; he did not fear anyone except Allah, and (during this journey) he would perform two rak‘ahs of prayer."
"343","It is narrated from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who states: I offered four rak'ahs of Zuhr prayer with the Prophet (ﷺ) in Madinah, and at the place of Dhu'l-Hulaifah, I offered two rak'ahs (shortened) of Asr prayer."
"344","It is narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir that he heard Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrate similar to the previous hadith."
"345","It is narrated in a similar manner from Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) through another chain of transmission."
"346","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that when he traveled a distance of one barid with Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he (Ibn ‘Umar) would not shorten the prayer during that journey."
"347","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he was asked: ""Will the prayer be shortened until (reaching) Arafah?"" He replied: ""No, rather (it will be shortened) in the case of travel to Asfan, Jeddah, and Taif."
"348","It is narrated from Salim bin Abdullah that Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) traveled towards ""Dhat al-Nusub,"" and during this journey, he shortened the prayer. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) says: There is a distance of four barid between Madinah and Dhat al-Nusub."
"349","It is narrated from Salim bin Abdullah that Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) rode towards Raym (the name of a place near Madinah), and he performed shortened prayers on this route. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) says: This too is located four burud from Madinah."
"350","It is narrated from Ata bin Abi Rabah (may Allah have mercy on him) that he states: I asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), ""Should I shorten the prayer until Arafah?"" He replied, ""No, rather until Jeddah, Usfan, and Taif; and if you come with your family or on foot, then pray in full."" Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) says: And this is also the statement of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and we will act upon it."
"351","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that he said: Prayer will be shortened up to ‘Usfan, Ta’if, and Jeddah. And all these places are at a distance of four barid from Makkah, and similarly, prayer will be shortened in places at such a distance."
"352","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he went towards Dhat al-Nusub and performed shortened (qasr) prayer. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) says: And this is at a distance of four barid."
"353","It is narrated from Ya'la bin Umayyah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I asked Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Allah Almighty has mentioned shortening (the prayer) in a state of fear, but where is the shortening besides fear?"" Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: ""I was also surprised by the same thing that surprised you. So I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: 'It is a charity that Allah has given to you, so accept His charity.'"
"354","It is narrated from Ya'la bin Umayyah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: I asked Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Indeed, Allah Almighty has said: 'Shorten the prayers if you fear that the disbelievers may harm you' [النساء: 101] but now people are in safety?"" So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I was also surprised at what you are surprised at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he (ﷺ) said: 'It is a charity, which Allah has given to you, so accept His charity.'"
"355","It is narrated from Ibn al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The best among you are those who, when they travel, shorten the prayer and break the fast,"" or he said: ""those who do not fast."
"356","Narrated by Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she stated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sometimes shortened the prayer during travel and sometimes performed it in full."
"357","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: At first, the prayer was made obligatory as two rak'ahs, then the prayers performed in residence were increased, and the prayer during travel was kept as it was. Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him) says: I asked, then why did Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) herself perform the full prayer during travel? Urwah replied: She adopted the same interpretation as Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"358","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that when he prayed behind the Imam in Mina, he would perform four (obligatory) units, and when he prayed by himself, he would perform two units."
"359","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that during travel he would not perform anything before or after the obligatory prayer, except for the Tahajjud prayer."
"360","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: ""Shall I not inform you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during travel? When he was at home and the sun had declined, he would combine Dhuhr and Asr at the time of decline. And if he set out on a journey before the sun's decline, he would delay Dhuhr until he combined Dhuhr and Asr at the time of Asr."" Kurayb (may Allah have mercy on him) says: According to my understanding, he also said the same regarding Maghrib and Isha."
"361","Mu'adh bin Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that he set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of Tabuk (on a journey). And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would combine the Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha prayers. He said: ""One day, he delayed the prayer, then went out and performed Zuhr and Asr together. Then he returned, and later set out again and performed Maghrib and Isha together."" [فوائد: 1. دورانِ سفر دو نمازیں جمع کر کے پڑھنا جائز ہے۔ 2. دورانِ سفر نمازوں میں تقدیم و تاخیر یعنی ظہر کو عصر کے وقت میں اور مغرب کو عشاء کے وقت میں یا عصر کو ظہر کے وقت اور عشاء کو مغرب کے وقت میں پڑھنا جائز ہے۔ 3. غزوہ تبوک شام کے عیسائیوں کے خلاف تھا جو مسلمانوں پر حملہ کرنا چاہتے تھے۔ یہ غزوہ رجب 9 ہجری میں ہوا اس میں مسلمانوں کی تعداد تیس ہزار تھی اس کو غزوۃ العسرۃ (تنگی کی جنگ) بھی کہا جاتا ہے۔ 4. معلوم ہوا خلیفہ بذات خود فوج کی کمانڈ کر سکتا ہے۔]"
"362","It is narrated from Ismail bin Abdur Rahman bin Abi Dhi’b Al-Asadi that he reports: We went with Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) towards the place of Jamaa, the sun had set, and we were afraid to say to him to dismount from the mounts so that we could pray. When the whiteness of the sky disappeared and the initial darkness of the night came, he dismounted from his mount and prayed three rak‘ahs, then gave salam, then prayed two rak‘ahs and gave salam, then addressing us he said: “I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do this.”"
"363","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he states: When the Prophet ﷺ needed to hasten during a journey, he ﷺ would combine Maghrib and Isha prayers."
"364","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates that when the Prophet ﷺ was in a hurry to travel, he would combine the Maghrib and Isha prayers."
"365","It is narrated from Mu'adh bin Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to combine the Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha prayers during his journey to Tabuk."
"366","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) combined the Zuhr and Asr prayers, and the Maghrib and Isha prayers, without fear or travel. Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) said: In my opinion, this was during rain."
"367","It is narrated from Abdur Rahman bin Humaid (may Allah have mercy on him) that he states: Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) asked his companions, ""What have you heard regarding the stay of a Muhajir in Makkah?"" Saib bin Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Ala bin Al-Hadrami (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""After performing the rites of Hajj, the Muhajir will stay for three days."
"368","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that at the place of Batn Nakhl (a settlement near Madinah), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led the people in the Zuhr prayer during a time of fear. He led one group in two rak‘ahs and then said salam. Then the second group came, and he led them in two rak‘ahs and then said salam."
"369","Salih bin Khawwat (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated from a person who offered the prayer of fear with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Dhat al-Riqa’. (That person stated) that one group formed rows with the Prophet ﷺ for prayer, and another group stood facing the enemy in rows. The Prophet ﷺ led those who stood with him in one rak‘ah, then he remained standing while they completed the rest of their prayer individually and left, going to stand in rows before the enemy. Then the second group came, and the Prophet ﷺ led them in the remaining rak‘ah and remained seated, while they completed the rest of their prayer during this time. Then the Prophet ﷺ said the salam with them."
"370","From another chain of narration, Khawat bin Jubair narrates from the Prophet ﷺ a hadith with the same meaning as the previous one, which does not contradict the first."
"371","It is narrated from Nafi‘ (may Allah have mercy on him) that when ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about the prayer of fear, he would say: A group will pray with the imam first, then he narrated the hadith (regarding the prayer of fear), and Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said in this hadith: ""If the danger is very great, then pray while walking and on your mount, facing the qiblah and not facing the qiblah (pray in any direction)."" Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) says that Nafi‘ said that ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated this from the Prophet ﷺ."
"372","With another chain of narration, Salim reports from his father Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) a narration similar to the previous hadith."
"373","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that he reported something different from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) regarding the Salat al-Khawf (prayer of fear), and Malik (may Allah have mercy on him) says that I only narrate it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). And Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrates it from Zuhri, Zuhri from Salim, and Salim from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), and Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) narrates from the Prophet (ﷺ), about which they have no doubt."
"374","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him), who narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he says: I think Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ), mentioning the prayer of fear, so he said: ""If the danger is severe, then pray while walking and on mounts, facing the qiblah or not facing the qiblah."
"375","Salim narrates from his father Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) a narration from the Prophet ﷺ similar in meaning to the previous hadith. And he has no doubt that it is narrated from his father and that it is attributed to the Prophet ﷺ as a marfu’ (elevated) narration."
"376","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would perform prayer on his camel during travel, whichever direction its face turned."
"377","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he stated: I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying on a donkey, while his face was towards Khaybar. Imam Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: That is, it was a voluntary (nafl) prayer."
"378","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performing voluntary prayers on a she-camel, facing every direction."
"379","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was praying on his camel facing towards the east during the expedition of Banu Anmar."
"380","It is narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was praying on his camel facing towards the east during the expedition of Banu Anmar."
"381","Abu Zubair narrates from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) a hadith similar to the previous one in a marfu’ form, except that he said: ""I do not know whether he mentioned the name of Banu Anmar or said that he prayed during a journey."
"382","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he spent a night at the house of the purified wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his maternal aunt, Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her). He said: I lay down across the pillow, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his wife lay down lengthwise. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept until, when half the night had passed or a little before or a little after, he woke up and began to wipe sleep from his face with his hands. Then he recited the last ten verses of Surah Al-Imran. Then he went to a water skin hanging (on a peg) and performed ablution from it thoroughly. Then he stood up and began to pray. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I also stood up and did as the Prophet ﷺ had done. Then I stood on his (left) side. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his right hand on my head and took hold of my right ear and twisted it. He prayed two rak‘ahs, then again two rak‘ahs, then again two rak‘ahs, then again two rak‘ahs, then again two rak‘ahs, then he prayed Witr and lay down. Until the muezzin came, then he got up and prayed two light rak‘ahs, then went out and led the morning prayer."
"383","Narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to pray eleven rak'ahs at night, including one rak'ah of Witr."
"384","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The night prayer is in sets of two rak'ahs, then when one fears that dawn is approaching, he should pray one rak'ah, and that will make all his prayer odd."
"385","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the night prayer, so the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The night prayer is in sets of two rak‘ahs. Then when one of you fears that dawn is approaching, he should pray one rak‘ah, and it will make all the prayer he has offered odd (witr)."
"386","It is narrated in a similar manner from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) through another chain of narration."
"387","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who states: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""The night prayer is in sets of two rak‘ahs. Then when one fears that dawn is approaching, he should pray one rak‘ah as witr."
"388","Through another chain, it is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet ﷺ in the same manner."
"389","Narrated by Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), she states that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) performed Witr in every part of the night, and in the latter part of his life, his Witr would (usually) end at the time of Sahar (pre-dawn)."
"390","Narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace be upon him) used to pray five rak'ahs of Witr, sitting for Tashahhud only in the last rak'ah, and then he would say the Salam."
"391","It is narrated from Ibn Shihab that Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform one rak'ah of Witr."
"392","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) who states: I was with Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) in Makkah and there were clouds covering the sky. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) feared that true dawn had appeared, so he performed one rak'ah of witr. Then the clouds cleared, and he saw that it was still night, so he made it even by performing another rak'ah."
"393","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when he performed three rak'ahs of Witr, would say salam after two rak'ahs and then would even engage in conversation if needed."
"394","Kuraib, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), narrates that he saw Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) perform the Isha prayer, and then he did not pray anything except one rak'ah of Witr. Kuraib (may Allah have mercy on him) related this to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), so he said: ""He has spoken the truth. O son! None of us knows more about this matter than Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him). This Witr can be one, or five, or more than seven. One may pray as many as he wishes."
"395","It is narrated from Sa’ib bin Yazid (may Allah have mercy on him) that a man asked Abdur Rahman al-Taimi about the prayer of Talha (may Allah be pleased with him), so Abdur Rahman al-Taimi said, ""If you wish, I can tell you about the prayer of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."" He said, ""One night, I was in a place when suddenly I got up and a man, who had his head covered, bumped into me. I looked and saw that it was Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). I stood behind him and he prayed, and he would perform prostrations of the Qur'an until I said: 'Fajr is about to break,' and he did not pray anything except one rak'ah of Witr."
"396","It is narrated from Hattan bin Abdullah (may Allah have mercy on him) who states that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Witr is of three types. Whoever among you wishes to perform it in the first part of the night, let him do so. Then, if he wakes up, he may, if he wishes, make it even by adding one rak'ah and then pray two by two rak'ahs until the true dawn appears. And if he wishes, he may perform witr in the last part of the night."
"397","It is narrated from Abdur Rahman bin Abd al-Qari (may Allah have mercy on him), he says: I heard Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stating that once I heard Hisham bin Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting Surah Al-Furqan in a manner different from the way I recite it. Although the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself had taught me that recitation. I was about to become angry with him, but then I gave him respite until he finished the prayer. When he finished, I wrapped his own cloak around his neck and dragged him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said, ""I have heard him reciting in a way different from what you taught me."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Hisham (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Recite!"" So he recited in the manner I had heard him recite. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""It was revealed in this way."" Then he said to me, ""You recite!"" So I recited, and he ﷺ said, ""It was revealed in this way also. Indeed, the Qur'an was revealed in seven dialects, so recite in whichever is easy for you."
"398","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) who states: I always heard Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting: ""Proceed to the remembrance of Allah."
